# Reading notes: arXiv 2610.02344v1 (for E5 of the standard)

De Melo Costa, Ahn, Popineau, Rimmel and Doan, *Drive vs. Decay: On the Training
Dynamics of Joint-Embedding Predictive Architectures*. v1 was submitted on
2026-10-01, before the 18 Oct 2026 cutoff of objective #5. The arXiv comment
says "Accepted at NeurIPS 2026 (Main Track)". The PDF has 57 pages. I read the
v1 PDF (arxiv.org/pdf/2610.02344v1) on 2026-10-17. Page numbers below are PDF
pages. These are my notes for judging the extension's positioning, not the
brief, which belongs to idea-repo.

## What it establishes (prior work the extension must credit)

- **Model (p3–4, Lemma 3.1):**
  - linear JEPA with context encoder W, EMA target W_a at rate β, linear
    predictor W_p;
  - Bernoulli feature masks on the context input and on the target outputs;
  - population gradient flow.
- **Criterion (p4, Eqs. 3–7):**
  - It linearises at W = W_a = 0 with the predictor held fixed.
  - Drive γ ∈ Spec(Σ_X1ᵀ ⊗ W_pᵀ M̄_2). Decay σ ∈ Spec(Σ_11 ⊗ W_pᵀ W_p).
  - Each mode has the characteristic equation λ² + (β+σ)λ + βσ(1−μ) = 0, with
    μ = γ/σ. A mode escapes iff μ > 1.
  - Under approximate spectral decoupling (Assumption 3.2),
    μ_kℓ = (c_k/a_k)(q_ℓ/p_ℓ): a data-side factor times a predictor-side factor.
  - App. A.5 gives non-decoupled certificates.
- **Rank budget (p5, Cor. 3.3):** the count of super-critical modes (k, ℓ)
  bounds the post-escape rank of the growing component and the effective rank.
  It is tested on 810 Tabular-JEPA runs, with ρ(ERank, r̃_tight) = 0.91 / 0.91 /
  0.62 on helena / aloi / jannis (p8, Table 2).
- **Joint training (p5, Lemma 3.4, App. F.6):**
  - The predictor gradient is quadratic in the encoder, so the collapsed set is
    a manifold of equilibria.
  - The predictor is quasi-static while the encoder grows from small
    initialisation, and the count of unstable eigenvalues is preserved up to
    O(δ²).
  - **There is no predictor weight decay in this lemma.**
- **Heuristics as doses on μ (p7–8, Table 3; App. J, p48–49):**
  - J.1, weight decay on the context encoder only: an additive floor,
    σ → σ + η_wd, so a mode escapes iff γ − σ > η_wd. This is static.
  - J.2, predictor scale α: μ(α) = μ(1)/α, and nothing escapes above
    α_ext = max μ(1).
  - J.3 and J.4: SIGReg and VICReg lower the predictor factor.
  - J.5: mimetic initialisation is a decaying dose.
  - J.6, frozen identity predictor: mode k escapes iff s_k > (1−p)/(p_tgt−p).
    With symmetric additive view noise nothing escapes.
  - J.7, EMA rate β: it sets the speed, not which modes escape, and the escape
    rate increases with β. The proportional-target assumption of Tian et al. is
    the limit β → ∞ (App. F).
  - Masking enters through the cross-view retention c_k/a_k.
- **Recovered as limiting cases (App. J.2):** DirectPred, IsoLoss, the rate
  equation of Halvagal et al. and predictor orthonormalisation.
- **ResidualPred (p9–10, App. I):**
  - an identity-biased attention predictor (W_p = I + ΔW_p at initialisation);
  - better rank and probe accuracy than the standard predictor on tabular data
    and in I-JEPA: ViT-S/4 on CIFAR-10/100, ViT-S/8 on STL-10, ViT-S/16 on
    ImageNet-100 at predictor depths 2 and 6, ViT-B/16, and an ImageNet-1k
    matched-budget pilot;
  - it beats SIGReg and the two compose;
  - mimetic initialisation trails it by 4.9 points.
- **Images (p10, App. K):**
  - The initialisation-time estimator does not separate the predictors.
  - The drive is measured after the predictor equilibrates: it is higher for
    ResidualPred in 9 of 9 matched comparisons.
  - Effective rank is necessary for accuracy but not sufficient.
- **Its stated limitations (p10):**
  - linear setting and spectral decoupling;
  - images are an empirical extension measured after predictor equilibration;
  - μ = 1 is a local initialisation criterion;
  - the full official I-JEPA recipe is untested.

## Overlap with the delivered paper (6e496ca)

| Delivered claim | Overlapping part of 2610.02344 |
|---|---|
| Per-direction drive against decay | its core (Eqs. 3–7) |
| Rank as the number of directions that escape | its Cor. 3.3 rank budget |
| Predictor scale p_0 | J.2 |
| EMA rate | J.7 |
| Masking through the statistics the masks leave | cross-view retention c_k/a_k, J.6 |
| Frozen target, identity predictor | J.6 |
| Weight decay | J.1, static floor on the encoder only |
| ViT tests | ResidualPred ViT experiments, App. K |

## Not in it, as far as this reading goes

- **Predictor weight decay and the decaying predictor gain.** Lemma 3.4 has a
  quasi-static predictor and no decay. In the delivered paper the race
  (survival when the growing direction captures the predictor before its gain
  decays out of the window), collapse as an attractor under weight decay, and
  the unbalanced start are the parts this work does not contain.
- The delivered paper's held-out pre-registered tests, its λ-collapse boundary
  against the static threshold, depth L, the learning-rate ratio α, AdamW, and
  the masking-race tests (E2).

## Consequences for the review (standard E5)

1. The extended paper must cite this work. Every delivered claim in the overlap
   table is re-worded as building on it, or as departing from it where it does
   (for example J.7 says EMA cannot change which modes escape; the delivered
   paper has EMA predictions, one of whose signs failed).
2. Its μ-count with the weight-decay floor (J.1) is a static, quasi-static
   predictor criterion. It is the natural baseline beside the static threshold
   on the delivered E1b/E1c/E2 runs and on any new runs. If the race beats it,
   that is the clearest statement of what is new. If not, the paper must say so.
3. Identity-biased predictors are prior work (ResidualPred). An extension
   claiming a predictor design must compare against ResidualPred.
4. Their App. K finding bears on O1: on images the initialisation-time drive
   estimator is null and the drive is read after predictor equilibration, which
   is consistent with the delivered E3 step-0 rule's partial failure.
